## The Week at a Glance

- 🟡 **Browser-based scam protection is coming to Edge:** Microsoft Edge will automatically block notifications from sites flagged as scams or phishing pages, reducing a common social-engineering entry point for your workforce, arriving in September 2026.
- 🟢 **Edge site troubleshooting gets easier for employees:** A new in-browser tool will help users resolve compatibility issues without IT involvement, potentially reducing help desk calls.
- 🟢 **Security advisory updates this week are informational only:** Three CVE entries were updated with corrected names or acknowledgements, with no new patches or exploited vulnerabilities disclosed.

*Sources: [Microsoft 365 Roadmap](https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-365/roadmap?id=568366) · [Microsoft Security Response Center](https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/vulnerability/CVE-2026-50422)*

---

## Why This Week Matters

This is a low-volume week with no emergency patches, identity changes, or compliance deadlines. The meaningful planning signal is the September arrival of automatic scam-notification blocking in Edge, which closes a low-visibility but persistent phishing vector. Leaders overseeing employee security awareness programs should note that browser-level automation will soon do some of the work that training alone cannot fully address.

*Sources: [Microsoft 365 Roadmap - Edge Scam Notification Blocking](https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-365/roadmap?id=568366)*

---

## Risk & Compliance

- 🟢 **[Edge Automatic Scam Notification Blocking](https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-365/roadmap?id=568366):** This feature reduces the risk of employees clicking through to phishing or malware sites via browser notifications; no compliance deadline applies, but organizations subject to HIPAA or SOC 2 should note it as a defense-in-depth improvement arriving in September.
- 🟢 **[CVE-2026-50422 - Windows NTFS Elevation of Privilege](https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/vulnerability/CVE-2026-50422):** This week's update is an acknowledgement correction only, with no new patch or exploitability change; confirm your patch management records reflect the correct metadata if your team tracks CVEs for compliance reporting.
- 🟢 **[CVE-2026-59117 - Windows Terminal Remote Code Execution](https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/vulnerability/CVE-2026-59117):** The affected product name was corrected from Microsoft Power Apps to Microsoft Power Apps Desktop Client; organizations that cited the original name in compliance documentation or risk registers should update their records.

*Sources: [Microsoft Security Response Center - CVE-2026-50422](https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/vulnerability/CVE-2026-50422) · [Microsoft Security Response Center - CVE-2026-59117](https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/vulnerability/CVE-2026-59117) · [Microsoft Security Response Center - CVE-2026-47301](https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/vulnerability/CVE-2026-47301)*

---

## What Your Employees Will Notice

- **Blocked notification pop-ups in Edge:** Starting in September, employees may notice that browser notification prompts from certain sites are silently removed, and they will receive an in-browser alert explaining why. Most employees will find this helpful, but a small number may question why a site they use regularly stopped sending notifications. Proactive communication before rollout will reduce confusion.
- **New troubleshooting prompts in Edge:** When a website behaves unexpectedly, Edge will surface context-sensitive guidance rather than leaving employees to navigate settings menus. This is a low-friction change that most users will experience as a quiet improvement.

*Sources: [Microsoft 365 Roadmap - Edge Scam Notification Blocking](https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-365/roadmap?id=568366) · [Microsoft 365 Roadmap - Edge Site Troubleshooting](https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-365/roadmap?id=561486)*

---

## What Your Help Desk Should Expect

- **Low volume week overall:** No new feature rollouts are landing this week, so ticket spikes are unlikely.
- **Advance questions about Edge notification changes:** As September approaches, employees who research the upcoming change may contact IT asking whether legitimate notification sources they rely on will be affected. Help desk staff should be briefed that users can review and restore any blocked notification source themselves through Edge settings, and should know where to direct those users.
- **CVE documentation corrections:** Security or GRC teams may submit requests to update internal records for the three CVEs with corrected metadata. These are low-urgency administrative updates.

*Sources: [Microsoft 365 Roadmap - Edge Scam Notification Blocking](https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-365/roadmap?id=568366) · [Microsoft Security Response Center](https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/vulnerability/CVE-2026-50422)*

---

## Planning Horizon

- **By September 2026: [Edge Automatic Scam Notification Blocking](https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-365/roadmap?id=568366):** Decide whether to issue proactive employee communication before rollout so that help desk volume stays low and employees understand the change as a security benefit, not a malfunction.
- **By September 2026: [Edge Site Troubleshooting Controls](https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-365/roadmap?id=561486):** Determine whether your organization's Edge management policies need to be reviewed to ensure the new in-browser troubleshooting prompts align with your approved browser configuration baseline.

*Sources: [Microsoft 365 Roadmap - Edge Scam Notification Blocking](https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-365/roadmap?id=568366) · [Microsoft 365 Roadmap - Edge Site Troubleshooting](https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-365/roadmap?id=561486)*

---

## If You Take No Action

- **Skipping employee communication on Edge notification blocking:** Employees who depend on browser notifications from third-party tools may report a sudden, unexplained loss of alerts. Without advance communication, help desk volume will spike in September as staff attempt to diagnose what they assume is a malfunction rather than a security feature.
- **Ignoring CVE metadata corrections in compliance records:** Organizations that track CVEs for SOC 2, CMMC, or cyber insurance purposes and do not update their records for CVE-2026-59117 will carry the wrong product name in their documentation, creating discrepancies that auditors may flag as a process gap.

*Sources: [Microsoft Security Response Center - CVE-2026-59117](https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/vulnerability/CVE-2026-59117) · [Microsoft 365 Roadmap - Edge Scam Notification Blocking](https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-365/roadmap?id=568366)*